2359,7,1124,37018,90,16,15170,17,2137,1124,37018,90,16,15170,18,2137,481,16,1124,1291,8,1124,50853,1124,2359,7,1124,37018,90,16,15170,21,2137,481,16,11,1124,37018,90,16,15170,19,92,1124,1291,8,284,1124,37018,90,16,15170,17,92,1124,50853,1124,37018,90,16,15170,21,92,488,1124,37018,90,16,15170,18,92,1124,50853,10293,16,8,488,10293,16,8,1124,50853,1124,37018,90,16,15170,19,92,284,1124,37018,90,16,15170,16,17,92,481,1124,37018,90,16,15170,18,92,481,1124,37018,90,16,15170,19,92,284,1124,37018,90,16,15170,16,17,92,481,1124,37018,90,19,15170,16,17,92,481,1124,37018,90,18,15170,16,17,92,284,1124,37018,90,16,481,220,19,481,220,18,15170,16,17,92,284,481,59,37018,90,21,15170,16,17,92,284,481,59,37018,90,16,15170,17,92,7110,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe set of points $(x,y,z)$ that satisfy\n\\[2x = 3y = -z\\]is a line.\n\nThe set of points $(x,y,z)$ that satisfy\n\\[6x = -y = -4z\\]is another line.\n\nFind the angle between these lines, in degrees.\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo find the angle between the two lines given by the equations \\(2x = 3y = -z\\) and \\(6x = -y = -4z\\), we first need to express these lines in parametric form.\n\nStarting with the first line \\(2x = 3y = -z\\), we can set \\(2x = 3y = -z = t\\). Then, we have:\n\\[x = \\frac{t}{2}, \\quad y = \\frac{t}{3}, \\quad z = -t.\\]\nSo, the parametric form of the first line is:\n\\[\\left( \\frac{t}{2}, \\frac{t}{3}, -t \\right) = t \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1 \\right).\\]\nThe direction vector of the first line is \\(\\mathbf{d_1} = \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1 \\right)\\).\n\nNext, for the second line \\(6x = -y = -4z\\), we can set \\(6x = -y = -4z = s\\). Then, we have:\n\\[x = \\frac{s}{6}, \\quad y = -s, \\quad z = \\frac{s}{4}.\\]\nSo, the parametric form of the second line is:\n\\[\\left( \\frac{s}{6}, -s, \\frac{s}{4} \\right) = s \\left( \\frac{1}{6}, -1, \\frac{1}{4} \\right).\\]\nThe direction vector of the second line is \\(\\mathbf{d_2} = \\left( \\frac{1}{6}, -1, \\frac{1}{4} \\right)\\).\n\nThe angle \\(\\theta\\) between the two lines is the same as the angle between their direction vectors \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{d_2}\\). The cosine of the angle between two vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is given by:\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}| |\\mathbf{b}|}.\\]\nFirst, we compute the dot product \\(\\mathbf{d_1} \\cdot \\mathbf{d_2}\\):\n\\[\\mathbf{d_1} \\cdot \\mathbf{d_2} = \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1 \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{1}{6}, -1, \\frac{1}{4} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (-1) + (-1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{4}{12} - \\frac{3}{12} = \\frac{1 - 4 - 3}{12} = -\\frac{6}{12} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"23b834d188d430e77e74563a87289c962f678416efd33388fd092c0e38c87090"},"seconds":36.610790967941284,"seed":367537462450872943,"stop":"eos","text":"Next, we compute the magnitudes of \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{d_2}\\):\n\\[|\\mathbf{d_1}| = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right)^2 + (-1)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{9} + 1} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{36} + \\frac{4}{36} + \\frac{36}{36}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} = \\frac{7}{6},\\]\n\\[|\\mathbf{d_2}| = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\right)^2 + (-1)^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{4} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36} + 1 + \\frac{1}{16}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{144} + \\frac{144}{144} + \\frac{9}{144}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{157}{144}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{157}}{12}.\\]\nNow, we can compute \\(\\cos \\theta\\):\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{d_1} \\cdot \\mathbf{d_2}}{|\\mathbf{d_1}| |\\mathbf{d_2}|} = \\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{\\frac{7}{6} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{157}}{12}} = \\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{\\frac{7\\sqrt{157}}{72}} = \\frac{-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 72}{7\\sqrt{157}} = \\frac{-36}{7\\sqrt{157}} = -\\frac{36}{7\\sqrt{157}}.\\]\nTo find the angle \\(\\theta\\), we take the inverse cosine:\n\\[\\theta = \\cos^{-1} \\left( -\\frac{36}{7\\sqrt{157}} \\right).\\]\nUsing a calculator, we find that \\(\\theta \\approx 117.07^\\circ\\).\n\nTherefore, the angle between the two lines is \\(\\boxed{117.07}\\) degrees."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{117.07}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Next, we compute the magnitudes of \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{d_2}\\):\n\\[|\\mathbf{d_1}| = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right)^2 + (-1)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{9} + 1} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{36} + \\frac{4}{36} + \\frac{36}{36}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} = \\frac{7}{6},\\]\n\\[|\\mathbf{d_2}| = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\right)^2 + (-1)^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{4} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36} + 1 + \\frac{1}{16}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{144} + \\frac{144}{144} + \\frac{9}{144}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{157}{144}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{157}}{12}.\\]\nNow, we can compute \\(\\cos \\theta\\):\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{d_1} \\cdot \\mathbf{d_2}}{|\\mathbf{d_1}| |\\mathbf{d_2}|} = \\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{\\frac{7}{6} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{157}}{12}} = \\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{\\frac{7\\sqrt{157}}{72}} = \\frac{-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 72}{7\\sqrt{157}} = \\frac{-36}{7\\sqrt{157}} = -\\frac{36}{7\\sqrt{157}}.\\]\nTo find the angle \\(\\theta\\), we take the inverse cosine:\n\\[\\theta = \\cos^{-1} \\left( -\\frac{36}{7\\sqrt{157}} \\right).\\]\nUsing a calculator, we find that \\(\\theta \\approx 117.07^\\circ\\).\n\nTherefore, the angle between the two lines is \\(\\boxed{117.07}\\) degrees.","id":"precalculus:515"}
